#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-janitor — the daily sweep: remove what is PROVEN dead, report everything else.

Tracks, branches and tmux windows outlive the work they were made for. Clearing them used to be something the
box owner noticed and asked for, which is the wrong shape for a chore that comes round every day (owner,
2026-08-31: "these cleanups should be regular tasks"). This is that pass, encoded, on a timer.

  cc-janitor <repo> [<repo> …] [--dry]      sweep exactly the repos named — never any other
  cc-janitor --configured [--dry]           the repos in CC_JANITOR_REPOS (~/.cc/config); the timer's form
  cc-janitor --fixtures [--dry]             rule 6 alone, no repo: cc-reconcile's 20-min pass calls this
  cc-janitor selfcheck                      the decision table + one end-to-end run, no network
    also: --no-net (never ask GitHub) · --dry (print, change nothing, write no log)

PER REPO, NEVER ACROSS THEM — rules 1-4. Those entry points take repo names: a tool that deletes has to be
pointed, and the timer is pointed by a config line the owner writes. Four passes are box-wide because what
they sweep points at itself rather than at a repo: rule 5 (a container names itself `ccbox-*`), rule 6 (a
fixture names itself `_cc<suite><pid>`), rule 7 (a process says where it is working and what that costs) and
rule 8 (the temp root is scratch by construction). All four run on every invocation, with an empty
CC_JANITOR_REPOS too.

THE ONE RULE ABOVE THE RULES: delete only what is PROVEN dead. Anything uncertain — a PR that closed without
merging, a branch nobody opened one for, a window with something running in it, a worktree with uncommitted
work — is REPORTED and left exactly where it is. This tool has no "probably".

  1. a board track `merged`/`done` for more than 48 h  →  `cc rm <repo> <track>`, which already takes the
     worktree, the tmux window, the local branch, the trust entry, the state dir and the board row. A track
     whose worktree still has uncommitted changes is reported instead — that is unsaved work, not a leftover.
  2. a remote branch `track/*` whose PR is MERGED  →  deleted on origin. No PR, or an OPEN or CLOSED one, is
     reported. GitHub is asked once per repo (`gh pr list`), and an answer that cannot be had is not evidence.
     An origin that cannot be listed is reported — except in a member workspace, which has none by design.
  3. a tmux window `<repo>/<track>` whose track is gone from the board (or terminal on it) AND whose pane is a
     bare shell  →  killed. `<repo>/<track>~next`, the leftover half of a handoff, follows its base track.
  4. never: the repo's own planning window (the one named `<repo>` exactly), a window with a live process, or
     anything whose track is queued/running/waiting/review/blocked. Only `merged` and `done` are ever swept.
  5. a ccbox container — named `ccbox-*`, status exited — stopped for CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS days (default 7,
     0 = off)  →  `docker rm`. Never `rmi`: the image stays. Only ccbox's own containers, by name, never a running
     one. A ccbox mounts one project dir and nothing else, so what a run meant to keep is already outside it;
     after a week the stopped container is a leftover, not a result.
  6. a test-suite fixture — board, lock, worktrees, state, ~/dev clone, scratch dir, deploy-ledger marker, context
     dir, tmux window, handoff-log lines, all named `_cc<suite><pid>` by the suite that made them (`_cctest<pid>` is
     core/tests/selftest.sh, `_ccsbx<pid>` cc-sandbox's selfchecks) — whose <pid> is gone → removed. THE PID IS THE
     WHOLE TEST: alive means the suite is running and everything it made is its. A suite that died before its own
     trap left a board whose `waiting` row reached the owner's NEEDS YOU list (2026-09-01), and a leaked `_ccsbx<pid>`
     that the board watchdog reported as two worktrees of real work (2026-09-02). A name under the prefix carrying no
     pid is never touched: the pid is the only proof of death this rule accepts.
  7. a process working in a SCRATCH WORKSPACE — one directory below the box's temp root (TMPDIR, else /tmp),
     which is scratch by construction: a run made it, and nothing anybody means to keep lives there — for longer
     than CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_HOURS (default 4, 0 = off), when the run that made it is gone AND it is still
     burning CPU  →  ended, TERM and then KILL, each one named. THE SESSION IS THE RUN: a process's session
     leader is the shell its run started in, so a leader that no longer exists is a run that no longer exists —
     whoever adopted the orphan afterwards (init, or a subreaper), and whatever the process happens to be
     called. Nothing here knows what a dev server is named. Two orphans are reported and left running instead:
     one in a workspace a REGISTERED RECORD points into, and one that is IDLE — a preview somebody is still
     reading sits on a port at no cost to anyone, while the class that cost a day of load was five dev servers
     holding half a core each with their test long gone and nothing on the box noticing (2026-09-06). Under
     CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_CPU (default 0.10 of a core, averaged over the process's whole life) nothing is proven
     but a leftover nobody is paying for, and that is a finding, not a kill.
  8. what those runs LEFT ON DISK — one entry below the temp root, a file or a directory and everything in it,
     that this user owns and NOTHING HAS WRITTEN TO for CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS days (default 7, 0 = off)  →
     removed. Rule 7 ends the processes; this is the half that fills a disk. A box running work all day leaves
     tens of thousands of these, nothing else on it removes them, and the first anyone hears of it is something
     failing for want of space. The age is the NEWEST mtime anywhere in the tree, never the top directory's: a
     directory nobody has added a name to for a month can hold a file written a minute ago. Two shapes are not
     leftovers and are never candidates — something this user does not own, and a tree holding anything that is
     not a plain file or directory: a socket, a fifo, a device, another filesystem mounted there. Those are
     doors onto something running, or somewhere else entirely, and a temp root is full of them; the socket
     every terminal session reaches its multiplexer through sits in a directory whose own clock stopped the day
     it was made.

NOTHING IS TAKEN OUT FROM UNDER SOMETHING STILL USING IT. Before any rule removes a directory, two generic
tests can hold it, and a held directory is REPORTED — "spared", and what holds it — never removed:
  · a live process whose working directory is inside it (`/proc/<pid>/cwd`, every pid this user can read: a cwd
    is a cwd, and this knows nothing about what the process is doing there or what project it belongs to);
  · a record pointing into it, in a file THE BOX HAS REGISTERED: CC_JANITOR_HOLDS in ~/.cc/config is whitespace-
    separated globs of JSON files, and every absolute path anywhere inside one holds the directory it names.
    This file registers none and never learns what a record is for — a box that serves something out of a
    worktree registers its own. A record nobody has rewritten for 48 h — the same window a terminal track gets
    — is a leftover like everything else here and holds nothing, so a writer that died cannot pin a worktree
    for ever; what is genuinely still running has a process, and the first test has it.
Rule 8 removes files rather than ending anything, so it takes the whole test AGAIN on each thing it is about to
remove: what it deletes on is a reading taken then, not one taken at the top of a pass that has been walking a
temp root since — the same second look rule 7 takes at a working directory before it signals.
Rule 7 is the one place the first test cannot be the evidence: the processes working in that directory are
exactly what it is judging, so there a registered record is the hold, and everything else it spares it spares
on what the processes themselves say — a live session, or an idle one.
A hold is on the RUN, not on the one path: rule 6 leaves that suite's board row, state, clone and window
alone too, because a live process with its bookkeeping deleted is the same accident in a smaller shape. A spare
is a standing state, so it is on stdout every pass but in the log once a day.
2026-09-05: a sweep took a worktree a dev server was serving out of. The server ran on with a deleted working
directory and rebuilt its cache inside the pruned path, which the board watchdog then read as work no board knew.

LIVENESS IS NOT DECIDED HERE. `cc-reconcile` already owns "what is really alive", and two definitions of that
would drift into a tool that kills working sessions. This reads its snapshot (~/.cc/state/reconcile.json,
refreshed first when stale) and treats `live` as final. The snapshot only covers tracks the board knows, so a
window for a track that was never on it falls back to the pane test `cc` itself uses (pane_live: the pane's
shell has a child) — the same rule, not a second one. Both must be quiet before anything is killed. Rule 6 is
outside all of this: a suite pid is not a track, so the snapshot has nothing to say about it and `/proc/<pid>`
is the whole test.

Every action goes to ~/.cc/state/janitor.log. `--dry` prints what it would do and writes nothing at all.
Exit: 0 done (with or without findings) · 2 usage.
"""
import calendar, glob, json, os, re, shutil, signal, stat, subprocess, sys, tempfile, time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))    # siblings come from the same bin/ as this copy: a worktree tests itself
BOARDS, WTS, STATE, DEV = f"{HOME}/.cc/boards", f"{HOME}/.cc/worktrees", f"{HOME}/.cc/state", f"{HOME}/dev"
LOG, SNAP = f"{STATE}/janitor.log", f"{STATE}/reconcile.json"
SPARES = f"{STATE}/janitor-spares.json"   # {target: when its spare was last logged} — a standing spare, once a day
STALE = 48 * 3600     # a terminal track younger than this is still being looked at — the owner reads a board, not a graveyard
SNAP_MAX = 3600       # cc-reconcile runs every 20 min; older than this is not evidence, so we ask it for a fresh one
PRMAX = 200           # one `gh pr list` per repo, capped: a timer job must never stall on the network
TERMINAL = ("merged", "done")
NEXT = "~next"        # cc-handoff's successor window suffix; `~` is not legal in a repo or track name
CONTAINER_DAYS = 7    # rule 5: a ccbox-* container stopped longer is a leftover; CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS overrides, 0 = off
FIXTURE = "_cc"       # rule 6: the prefix every suite's scratch names share, whichever suite made them…
SUITE = re.compile(r"_cc[a-z]+(\d+)(?:[/~]|$)")   # …and each carries its run's pid: `_cctest<pid>`, `_ccsbx<pid>/t`
ORPHAN_HOURS = 4      # rule 7: a process in a scratch workspace younger than this is somebody's run in progress
ORPHAN_CPU = 0.10     # …and one under this share of a core, over its whole life, is a leftover costing nothing
GRACE = 5             # …seconds between the TERM that asks it to stop and the KILL that does not ask
SCRATCH_DAYS = 7      # rule 8: a scratch entry nothing has written to for longer is a leftover; 0 = off
HZ = 100              # clock ticks per second, for /proc/<pid>/stat — the real one below, this if it cannot be had
try:
    HZ = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK") or HZ
except (ValueError, OSError):
    pass


def now():
    return time.time()


def epoch(ts):
    """Board timestamps are UTC '2026-08-30T21:01:43Z'; anything else is 'long ago', never 'in the future'."""
    try:
        return float(calendar.timegm(time.strptime(str(ts), "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")))
    except Exception:
        return 0.0


def dur(s):
    return f"{int(s // 86400)}d" if s >= 86400 else f"{int(s // 3600)}h"


# ── the decisions: pure, so the selfcheck drives every branch with no box around it ──────────────────────
def track_verdict(f):
    """Rule 1. f: {status, age, dirty, snap} → (rm|report|keep, why). `snap` is cc-reconcile's row for this
    track, or None when it has none. Age first, so a track that merged an hour ago is simply young rather
    than a finding: the noisy half of a report is the half nobody reads."""
    if f["status"] not in TERMINAL:
        return ("keep", "")                             # rule 4: only merged and done are ever swept
    if f["age"] <= f.get("stale", STALE):
        return ("keep", "")
    snap = f.get("snap")
    if snap and snap.get("live"):
        return ("report", "still alive — a session is in it")
    if snap and snap.get("state") not in TERMINAL:
        return ("report", f"the board says {f['status']}, cc-reconcile says {snap.get('state')}")
    if f["dirty"]:
        return ("report", "uncommitted changes in its worktree")
    return ("rm", f"{f['status']} for {dur(f['age'])}")


def branch_verdict(f):
    """Rule 2. f: {board, pr, dirty} → (delete|report|keep, why). `board` is the track's board status, '' when
    the board has never heard of it; `pr` is MERGED/OPEN/CLOSED, '?' when GitHub could not answer, None when
    no PR exists. Only MERGED is proof; everything else is somebody's unfinished business."""
    if f["board"] and f["board"] not in TERMINAL:
        return ("keep", "")                             # rule 4
    if f["dirty"]:
        return ("report", "its worktree has uncommitted changes")
    if f["pr"] == "MERGED":
        return ("delete", "its PR is merged")
    if f["pr"] in ("OPEN", "CLOSED"):
        return ("report", f"its PR is {f['pr'].lower()}")
    if f["pr"] == "?":
        return ("report", "its PR state could not be read")
    return ("report", "no PR was ever opened for it")


def window_verdict(f):
    """Rules 3 and 4. f: {board, bare} → (kill|report|keep, why). `bare` is already the AND of cc-reconcile's
    liveness and the pane test — one of them saying "alive" is enough to keep it."""
    if f["board"] and f["board"] not in TERMINAL:
        return ("keep", "")                             # rule 4
    if not f["bare"]:
        return ("report", "a live process is still in it")
    return ("kill", "a bare shell on a track that is " + (f["board"] or "not on the board"))


def container_verdict(f):
    """Rule 5. f: {name, status, age, days} → (rm|report|keep, why). `age` is seconds since docker's FinishedAt,
    None when that could not be read — and None is NOT "long ago" here: an unreadable clock holds the sweep.
    Only ccbox's own containers, by name; only exited ones; young is not a finding."""
    if not f["name"].startswith("ccbox-") or f["status"] != "exited":
        return ("keep", "")
    if f["age"] is None:
        return ("report", "when it stopped could not be read")
    if f["age"] < f.get("days", CONTAINER_DAYS) * 86400:
        return ("keep", "")
    return ("rm", f"a ccbox container stopped for {dur(f['age'])}")


def orphan_verdict(f):
    """Rule 7. f: {ws, age, cpu, run, held, hours, share} → (end|report|keep, why). The verdict is on the WORKSPACE,
    one for everything working in it: those processes are one run, and ending half of a run is how a sweep leaves a
    mess behind it. `age` is the oldest holder's, `cpu` the biggest share of a core any of them has held since it
    started, `run` names a holder's session leader that is still alive (the run is still going), `held` the
    registered records pointing in. Age first: a workspace an hour old is a run in progress, not a finding."""
    if f["age"] < f.get("hours", ORPHAN_HOURS) * 3600:
        return ("keep", "")
    if f["held"]:
        return ("report", f"spared {f['ws']}: " + "; ".join(f["held"]))
    if f["run"]:
        return ("report", f"spared {f['ws']}: the run that made it is still alive — {f['run']}")
    if f["cpu"] < f.get("share", ORPHAN_CPU):
        return ("report", f"spared {f['ws']}: its run is gone, but it is idle ({f['cpu'] * 100:.0f}% of a core) "
                          f"and costs nobody anything — end it by hand if it is finished with")
    return ("end", f"working in {f['ws']} for {dur(f['age'])} at {f['cpu'] * 100:.0f}% of a core, "
                   "and the run that made it is gone")


def leftover_verdict(f):
    """Rule 8. f: {path, ours, plain, age, held, days} → (rm|report|keep, why). `age` is the seconds since
    anything anywhere in it was last written, `plain` False when it holds something that is not a plain file or
    directory, `held` the reasons it must stay. Ours and plain first, and both silently: another user's file is
    not this box's to judge and a socket is a door onto something running, so neither is a finding — a temp root
    holds dozens of each, and a daily report listing them all is a report nobody reads."""
    if not f["ours"] or not f["plain"]:
        return ("keep", "")
    if f["age"] < f.get("days", SCRATCH_DAYS) * 86400:
        return ("keep", "")
    if f["held"]:
        return ("report", f"spared {f['path']}: " + "; ".join(f["held"]))
    return ("rm", f"nothing has written in it for {dur(f['age'])}")


# ── reality ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def board_read(path):
    """A board file, read through cc-board's opener (imported, the way cc-time is): a member's board is a link into a
    directory the MEMBER writes, and a plain open here followed whatever it planted — a pipe hung this tool for good
    (review of #224). None = no board there; cc-board's Refused for anything planted, which a caller treats as unreadable."""
    if not hasattr(board_read, "mod"):
        import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
        s = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("ccboard", importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
            "ccboard", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-board")))   # beside THIS copy, not a stubbed BIN
        board_read.mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(board_read.mod)
    return board_read.mod.read(path)


def board(repo):
    try:
        return board_read(f"{BOARDS}/{repo}.json")
    except Exception:
        return None


def terminal_since(d, track, t):
    """When the track's status LAST became merged/done. The board's own `updated` is bumped by every note too,
    so a merged track somebody commented on would look days younger than it is; the log says when the word
    actually changed. Falls back to `updated` for a board written before that entry, or migrated."""
    for e in reversed(d.get("log") or []):
        if e.get("track") == track and str(e.get("msg", "")).startswith("status -> "):
            return epoch(e.get("ts")) if e["msg"][len("status -> "):].strip() in TERMINAL else 0.0
    return epoch(t.get("updated") or t.get("created"))


def snapshot(refresh=True):
    """cc-reconcile's read-only view of what is alive. Refreshed first when stale — `snapshot` is a local read
    that asks nothing of the network. NOT under --dry, which writes nothing anywhere, not even another tool's
    cache; a preview that finds the snapshot stale says so instead, because without it this cannot see who is
    alive and its verdicts would be more confident than the real pass. Returns (tracks, stale)."""
    if os.environ.get("CC_JANITOR_NO_SNAP"):        # tests: the no-snapshot path must be reachable on demand
        return {}, True
    try:
        fresh = now() - os.stat(SNAP).st_mtime < SNAP_MAX
    except OSError:
        fresh = False
    if not fresh and refresh:
        try:
            subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-reconcile", "snapshot"], capture_output=True, timeout=120)
            fresh = now() - os.stat(SNAP).st_mtime < SNAP_MAX
        except Exception:
            pass
    try:
        return (json.load(open(SNAP)).get("tracks") or {}), not fresh
    except Exception:
        return {}, True


def dirty(wt):
    """Uncommitted work in a worktree — untracked files included, which is exactly the case that stings: a
    worker that wrote a file and never added it. A path with no `.git` in it is not a worktree at all (gone, or
    an empty shell `cc rm` will take): git holds nothing there, so there is nothing to lose. Anything git
    itself refuses to answer about counts as work in progress and gets reported."""
    if not os.path.exists(f"{wt}/.git"):
        return False
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", wt, "status", "--porcelain"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except Exception:
        return True
    return p.returncode != 0 or bool(p.stdout.strip())


def inside(d, p):
    """`p` is `d` itself or something under it. Both are absolute and already normalised."""
    d = d.rstrip("/")
    return p == d or p.startswith(d + "/")


def live_cwds():
    """[(pid, cwd)] for every process on this box whose working directory can be read. /proc is the whole test.
    A link that will not read — the process exited between the glob and the readlink, or it is another user's —
    is skipped: it is not evidence either way, and this box's own work all runs as one user."""
    out = []
    for d in glob.glob("/proc/[0-9]*"):
        try:
            out.append((os.path.basename(d), os.path.realpath(os.readlink(f"{d}/cwd"))))
        except OSError:
            continue
    return out


def pcomm(pid):
    """The process's own name, for the report: `pid 3117 (node)` says more than a number does."""
    try:
        return open(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read().strip() or "?"
    except OSError:
        return "?"


def boot():
    """When this kernel booted, so a process's start time in clock ticks becomes a wall clock one. 0 when /proc/stat
    will not say, and then nothing has a readable age — which rule 7 treats as no evidence, not as "old"."""
    if not hasattr(boot, "t"):
        boot.t = 0.0
        try:
            boot.t = next(float(ln.split()[1]) for ln in open("/proc/stat") if ln.startswith("btime "))
        except Exception:
            boot.t = 0.0
    return boot.t


def proc_stat(pid):
    """(ppid, session, started, cpu_seconds) from /proc/<pid>/stat, or None when it cannot be read. The comm field
    is parenthesised and holds whatever the process is called — spaces and brackets included — so the fields are
    counted from the LAST ')', never split off the front. `session` is the pid of the process's session leader:
    the shell its run started in, alive or gone. A kernel that will not say when it booted leaves every start time
    unreadable rather than wrong — an age counted from the epoch would make everything on the box look ancient."""
    try:
        if not boot():
            return None
        f = open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read().rpartition(")")[2].split()
        started = boot() + int(f[19]) / HZ                  # field 22, ticks since boot
        return (int(f[1]), int(f[3]), started, (int(f[11]) + int(f[12])) / HZ)   # ppid, session, start, utime+stime
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def ours(pid):
    """This user's own process. Another user's is nothing this sweep may end, and on a box that runs as one user
    there is nothing of ours behind that line anyway."""
    try:
        return os.stat(f"/proc/{pid}").st_uid == os.getuid()
    except OSError:
        return False


def own_chain():
    """This process and every process above it, up to init. Rule 7 never looks at one of these, wherever it is
    working: a sweep that could end its own shell, its session or the thing that started it is not a sweep."""
    out, pid = {1}, os.getpid()
    while pid > 1 and pid not in out:
        out.add(pid)
        st = proc_stat(pid)
        pid = st[0] if st else 0
    return out


def scratch(cwd, tmp):
    """The scratch workspace `cwd` is working in: the ONE directory below the box's temp root. The root itself is
    not a workspace — a process sitting in /tmp made nothing — and a path outside it is not one at all."""
    tmp = os.path.realpath(tmp).rstrip("/")
    if not inside(tmp, cwd) or cwd.rstrip("/") == tmp:
        return None
    return f"{tmp}/{cwd[len(tmp) + 1:].split('/')[0]}"


def scratch_scan(path, dev):
    """One entry below the temp root, measured: {newest, bytes, plain, ours}, or None when it is not a tree this
    sweep judges at all. `newest` is the last mtime ANYWHERE inside it, `bytes` what it is holding, `plain` False
    when anything in it is not a plain file or directory — a socket, a fifo, a device, or a name on another
    filesystem, which is a mount rather than something a run wrote. A symlink at the top is not a tree either:
    following one would take this sweep out of the temp root entirely."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode) or st.st_dev != dev:
        return None
    f = {"newest": st.st_mtime, "bytes": st.st_size, "plain": True, "ours": st.st_uid == os.getuid()}
    if stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        return f
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        return dict(f, plain=False)
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(path, onerror=lambda e: None):     # never follows a link out of the tree
        for name in dirs + files:
            try:
                t = os.lstat(os.path.join(root, name))
            except OSError:
                continue                                    # gone while this was reading is not a shape
            if t.st_dev != dev or not (stat.S_ISREG(t.st_mode) or stat.S_ISDIR(t.st_mode) or stat.S_ISLNK(t.st_mode)):
                return dict(f, plain=False)
            f["newest"] = max(f["newest"], t.st_mtime)
            f["bytes"] += t.st_size
    return f


def remove_path(p):
    """One leftover off disk: a file, or a directory and everything under it. '' when it is gone, why not
    otherwise. `rmtree` refuses a symlink and never follows one, which is the whole of what could reach outside."""
    try:
        if os.path.isdir(p) and not os.path.islink(p):
            shutil.rmtree(p)
        else:
            os.remove(p)
        return ""
    except OSError as e:
        return str(e)


def size(n):
    """Bytes for a person to read: a sweep's report is how much room it actually made."""
    for unit, div in (("GB", 1 << 30), ("MB", 1 << 20), ("kB", 1 << 10)):
        if n >= div:
            return f"{n / div:.1f} {unit}"
    return f"{int(n)} B"


def end_proc(pid, ws, grace=GRACE):
    """Ask, then insist: SIGTERM, and SIGKILL whatever is still running when the grace is up. Returns '' when the
    process is gone and why not otherwise. Already gone counts as gone — this pass's job, done by somebody else —
    and so does a zombie: it is not running, and reading it is its parent's business, not this sweep's.

    The working directory is read ONE more time first. Between the pass reading /proc and this signal the process
    can have exited and its pid number been handed to something else, and a pid that has moved on is a stranger:
    this is the only rule here that ends a thing it cannot get back, so it looks again rather than trusting a
    reading it took a moment ago."""
    try:
        if not inside(ws, os.path.realpath(os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd"))):
            return f"it is working somewhere else now, not in {ws} — nothing was sent"
    except OSError:
        return ""                                   # gone between the reading and here: nothing left to end

    def gone():
        try:
            return open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read().rpartition(")")[2].split()[0] == "Z"
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            return True
    for sig, wait in ((signal.SIGTERM, grace), (signal.SIGKILL, 2)):
        try:
            os.kill(pid, sig)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            return ""
        except OSError as e:
            return str(e)
        end = now() + wait
        while now() < end:
            if gone():
                return ""
            time.sleep(0.1)
    return "" if gone() else "it did not end"


def jstrings(v):
    """Every string in a JSON value, however deep: a record's shape is the box's business, not this file's."""
    if isinstance(v, str):
        yield v
    elif isinstance(v, dict):
        for x in v.values():
            yield from jstrings(x)
    elif isinstance(v, list):
        for x in v:
            yield from jstrings(x)


def record_paths():
    """[(target, record)] — every directory a REGISTERED record points at. CC_JANITOR_HOLDS is whitespace-
    separated globs of JSON files; every absolute path inside one is a pointer. A record older than STALE is
    not evidence any more, and one that is not JSON holds nothing: a hold is something a box says now."""
    out = []
    for pat in setting("CC_JANITOR_HOLDS").split():
        for rec in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.expanduser(pat))):
            try:
                if now() - os.stat(rec).st_mtime > STALE:
                    continue
                d = json.load(open(rec))
            except Exception:
                continue
            out += [(os.path.realpath(v), rec) for v in jstrings(d) if v.startswith("/")]
    return out


class Holds:
    """What is still using a directory — the process table and the box's registered records, read once per run
    (see the header). `on(path)` is why that path must not be removed, [] when nothing holds it."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.cwds = live_cwds()
        self.records = record_paths()

    def records_on(self, path):
        """…the registered records alone. Rule 7's candidates ARE the processes working in the directory, so the
        process test cannot be its evidence there; a record still is."""
        d = os.path.realpath(path)
        return [f"{rec} points into it" for tgt, rec in self.records if inside(d, tgt)]

    @staticmethod
    def why(pids, records):
        """The reasons, worded the same wherever they are read: two processes and a count of the rest — a dev
        server is a dozen processes — then the records, which come already worded."""
        why = [f"pid {pid} ({pcomm(pid)}) is working in it" for pid in pids[:2]]
        if len(pids) > 2:
            why.append(f"+{len(pids) - 2} more are working in it")
        return list(dict.fromkeys(why + list(records)))

    def on(self, path):
        d = os.path.realpath(path)
        return self.why([pid for pid, c in self.cwds if inside(d, c)], self.records_on(path))

    def under(self, tmp):
        """{scratch workspace: why} for everything held inside the temp root, in ONE walk of the readings. Rule 8
        looks at every name in that root, and asking each of them about every process would be the slow half of
        the day's sweep; a working directory or a record path below the root already names the one workspace it
        holds (rule 7's `scratch`), so one pass over the readings answers for all of them."""
        pids, recs = {}, {}
        for pid, cwd in self.cwds:
            ws = scratch(cwd, tmp)
            if ws:
                pids.setdefault(ws, []).append(pid)
        for tgt, rec in self.records:
            ws = scratch(tgt, tmp)
            if ws:
                recs.setdefault(ws, []).append(f"{rec} points into it")
        return {ws: self.why(pids.get(ws, []), dict.fromkeys(recs.get(ws, [])))
                for ws in set(pids) | set(recs)}


def windows():
    """[(id, name)] in tmux session `main` — addressed by id, because a name with a `/` in it breaks `-t`."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-windows", "-t", "main", "-F", "#{window_id}\t#{window_name}"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except Exception:
        return []
    out = []
    for ln in p.stdout.splitlines():
        wid, _, name = ln.partition("\t")
        if wid and name:
            out.append((wid, name))
    return out


def pane_bare(wid):
    """`cc`'s own pane_live test, negated: the wrapper bash is always the pane command, so a window holding a
    session or a worker is one whose pane shell HAS A CHILD. No child, nothing is running in there."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["tmux", "list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        pid = (p.stdout.splitlines() or [""])[0].strip()
        if not pid:
            return False                                # no pane to read → not proven bare
        return subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-P", pid], capture_output=True, timeout=20).returncode != 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def pr_states(repo, net=True):
    """{branch: MERGED|OPEN|CLOSED} for the repo, in ONE call. A branch absent from the map has no PR; when
    GitHub could not be asked at all the map is None, and every branch reads '?' — an unreachable GitHub is
    not evidence about a branch, and this deletes nothing on it."""
    cwd = f"{DEV}/{repo}"
    if not net or not shutil.which("gh") or not os.path.isdir(cwd):
        return None
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "list", "--state", "all", "--limit", str(PRMAX),
                            "--json", "number,state,headRefName"], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        rows = json.loads(p.stdout) if p.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        rows = None
    if rows is None:
        return None
    out = {}
    for r in rows:                                      # newest first: an older PR on the same branch never overwrites
        out.setdefault(r.get("headRefName") or "", str(r.get("state", "")).upper())
    return out


def remote_tracks(repo):
    """The `track/*` branches on origin, or None when origin could not be listed (no remote, no network)."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", f"{DEV}/{repo}", "ls-remote", "--heads", "origin", "refs/heads/track/*"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return [ln.split("refs/heads/")[-1] for ln in p.stdout.splitlines() if "refs/heads/track/" in ln]


def stopped_at(ts):
    """docker's FinishedAt, '2026-08-30T01:44:43.257081924Z' → epoch, or None when it cannot be read."""
    e = epoch(str(ts).split(".")[0].rstrip("Z") + "Z")
    return e if e > 0 else None


def containers():
    """ccbox's stopped containers, [(name, status, finished_epoch|None)], or None when docker could not be asked.
    `ps` picks them (name ^ccbox-, status exited); `inspect` gives the exact stop — its "2 days ago" is prose,
    not a clock. Plain `docker`, as cc-audit's leftovers check asks it: same user, same answer."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["docker", "ps", "-a", "--filter", "name=^ccbox-", "--filter", "status=exited",
                            "--format", "{{.Names}}"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        return None
    names = [n.strip() for n in p.stdout.splitlines() if n.strip()]
    if not names:
        return []
    try:
        q = subprocess.run(["docker", "inspect", "--format", "{{.Name}}\t{{.State.Status}}\t{{.State.FinishedAt}}",
                            *names], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except Exception:
        return None
    out = []
    for ln in q.stdout.splitlines():                    # a row that is there is real, even if another name went missing
        name, _, rest = ln.partition("\t")
        status, _, fin = rest.partition("\t")
        if name.strip():
            out.append((name.strip().lstrip("/"), status.strip(), stopped_at(fin.strip())))
    return out


# ── the run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Run:
    """One sweep. Holds the log handle, the dry-run flag and the tally, so every rule reports the same way."""

    def __init__(self, dry=False):
        self.dry, self.did, self.said = dry, [], []
        self.holds = Holds()            # one reading of /proc and of the registered records, for the whole pass

    def log(self, repo, verb, target, why):
        line = f"{time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())} {repo} {verb} {target} — {why}"
        if self.dry:
            print(f"  would {verb} {target} — {why}")
            return
        print(f"  {verb} {target} — {why}")
        try:
            os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
            with open(LOG, "a") as f:
                f.write(line + "\n")
        except OSError:
            pass                                        # a log nobody could write must not stop the sweep

    def act(self, repo, verb, target, why, run):
        """Do one thing, or say what it would have been. A failure is logged and the sweep carries on: one
        stuck worktree must not leave the other nine behind it unswept."""
        if self.dry:
            self.log(repo, verb, target, why)
            self.did.append(target)
            return True
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(run, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)
        except Exception as e:
            p = None
            err = str(e)
        if p is not None and p.returncode == 0:
            self.log(repo, verb, target, why)
            self.did.append(target)
            return True
        err = (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip().splitlines()[-1] if p is not None and (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip() else \
            (err if p is None else f"exit {p.returncode}")
        self.report(repo, target, f"{verb} failed: {err}")
        return False

    def act_here(self, repo, verb, target, why, fn):
        """`act`, for the one thing this file does itself rather than by running a command: rule 7's signal. `fn`
        returns '' when it worked and why not otherwise, and a failure is reported exactly like a command's."""
        if not self.dry:
            err = fn()
            if err:
                self.report(repo, target, f"{verb} failed: {err}")
                return False
        self.log(repo, verb, target, why)
        self.did.append(target)
        return True

    def report(self, repo, target, why):
        """A spare is a standing state, not an event: it is on stdout every pass, and in the log once a day.
        cc-reconcile calls `--fixtures` every 20 minutes, and one held directory would otherwise write 72
        identical lines a day into a log somebody reads to find out what happened."""
        self.said.append((target, why))
        if why.startswith("spared") and not self.dry and not self.spare_due(target):
            print(f"  report {target} — {why}")
            return
        self.log(repo, "report", target, why)

    def spare_due(self, target):
        """True when this target's spare has not been logged for a day — and records that it now has. A target
        nobody has spared for a week is forgotten, so the file stays the size of what is actually held."""
        try:
            seen = json.load(open(SPARES))
        except Exception:
            seen = {}
        if now() - float(seen.get(target, 0) or 0) < 86400:
            return False
        seen[target] = now()
        seen = {k: v for k, v in seen.items() if now() - float(v or 0) < 7 * 86400}
        try:
            os.makedirs(STATE, exist_ok=True)
            with open(f"{SPARES}.tmp", "w") as f:
                json.dump(seen, f)
            os.replace(f"{SPARES}.tmp", SPARES)
        except OSError:
            pass                                        # a note nobody could write must not stop the sweep
        return True


def paused(target):
    """`cc-pause on <repo>` parks a whole project — its repo, its tracks and its orchs — and nothing the box does
    on its own may spend on it until `cc-pause off`. cc-pause owns that state; asking it is the only reading of it
    there is. It cannot run (no such sibling on this box, a timeout): behave exactly as this file did before the
    pause existed — a pause must never be the reason a pass stops working."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-pause", "is", str(target)], stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              capture_output=True, timeout=10).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def sweep(repo, r, net=True, stale=STALE):
    """One repo, the four rules in order. Rule 1 first, because `cc rm` takes the window and the board row with
    it — so rules 2 and 3 read a board that has already had the dead swept out of it."""
    # One repo, so a pause is refused here rather than in main(): both doors — the timer's `--configured` form
    # and an explicit `cc-janitor <repo>` — come through this function, and rules 5 and 6 (containers, fixtures)
    # are deliberately box-wide and stay untouched by one project being parked.
    if paused(repo):
        print(f"cc-janitor {repo}: paused — skipped (cc-pause off {repo})")
        return
    print(f"cc-janitor {repo}{' (dry run)' if r.dry else ''}")
    d = board(repo)
    if d is None:
        r.report(repo, repo, "no board — nothing to sweep")
        return
    snap, no_snap = snapshot(refresh=not r.dry)     # NOT `stale`: that name is this function's 48 h threshold
    if no_snap:
        print("  note: cc-reconcile's snapshot is stale — this pass cannot see which tracks are still alive,")
        print("        so nothing is removed or killed this pass: report-only, the next fresh snapshot sweeps")

    # 1 — terminal tracks
    for track, t in sorted((d.get("tracks") or {}).items()):
        wt = t.get("worktree") or f"{WTS}/{repo}/{track}"
        f = {"status": t.get("status", ""), "age": max(0.0, now() - terminal_since(d, track, t)),
             "snap": snap.get(f"{repo}/{track}"), "stale": stale,
             "dirty": dirty(wt) if t.get("status", "") in TERMINAL else False}
        verdict, why = track_verdict(f)
        if verdict == "rm" and no_snap:
            verdict, why = "report", f"would rm ({why}) — held: no fresh liveness snapshot"
        if verdict == "rm":
            wid = next((w for w, n in windows() if n == f"{repo}/{track}"), "")
            if wid and not pane_bare(wid):
                verdict, why = "report", "would rm — held: its window is running something"
        if verdict == "rm":
            spared = r.holds.on(wt)     # `cc rm` takes the worktree with the row: not while somebody is in it
            if spared:
                verdict, why = "report", f"spared {wt}: " + "; ".join(spared)
        if verdict == "rm":
            r.act(repo, "rm", f"{repo}/{track}", why, [f"{BIN}/cc", "rm", repo, track])
        elif verdict == "report":
            r.report(repo, f"{repo}/{track}", why)

    # 2 — remote branches. The board is re-read: rule 1 has just taken rows off it.
    d = board(repo) or d
    tracks = d.get("tracks") or {}
    branches = remote_tracks(repo)
    if branches is None:
        # …unless the repo HAS no origin by design: a member workspace is local-only (docs/design-member-workspaces.md),
        # so "could not be listed" is the expected answer there, not news. It was reported for every member, every Sunday.
        if not os.path.exists(f"{DEV}/{repo}/.cc/member-workspace"):
            r.report(repo, f"origin/{repo}", "origin could not be listed — no branch was touched")
    else:
        prs = pr_states(repo, net)
        for b in sorted(branches):
            track = b[len("track/"):]
            t = tracks.get(track) or {}
            f = {"board": t.get("status", ""), "pr": "?" if prs is None else prs.get(b),
                 "dirty": dirty(t.get("worktree") or f"{WTS}/{repo}/{track}")}
            verdict, why = branch_verdict(f)
            if verdict == "delete":
                r.act(repo, "delete", f"origin/{b}", why, ["git", "-C", f"{DEV}/{repo}", "push", "origin", "--delete", b])
            elif verdict == "report":
                r.report(repo, f"origin/{b}", why)

    # 3 and 4 — tmux windows. The planning window is named `<repo>` exactly and is never a candidate here.
    for wid, name in windows():
        if not name.startswith(f"{repo}/"):
            continue
        track = name[len(repo) + 1:]
        if track.endswith(NEXT):                        # a handoff successor follows its base track's rule
            track = track[:-len(NEXT)]
        if not track:
            continue
        t = tracks.get(track) or {}
        alive = (snap.get(f"{repo}/{track}") or {}).get("live")
        f = {"board": t.get("status", ""), "bare": not alive and pane_bare(wid)}
        verdict, why = window_verdict(f)
        if verdict == "kill":
            r.act(repo, "kill", f"window {name}", why, ["tmux", "kill-window", "-t", wid])
        elif verdict == "report":
            r.report(repo, f"window {name}", why)


def sweep_containers(r, days):
    """Rule 5, once per run and box-wide: a ccbox is named for a ~/dev project, not for a repo on a board, so
    `ccbox-` is the pointing. A box without docker has no ccbox and hears nothing about it."""
    if days <= 0 or not shutil.which("docker"):
        return
    print(f"cc-janitor ccbox containers{' (dry run)' if r.dry else ''}")
    rows = containers()
    if rows is None:
        r.report("ccbox", "docker", "docker could not be asked — no container was touched")
        return
    for name, status, fin in sorted(rows):
        f = {"name": name, "status": status, "days": days, "age": None if fin is None else max(0.0, now() - fin)}
        verdict, why = container_verdict(f)
        if verdict == "rm":
            r.act("ccbox", "rm", f"container {name}", why, ["docker", "rm", name])
        elif verdict == "report":
            r.report("ccbox", f"container {name}", why)


def sweep_orphans(r, hours, share=ORPHAN_CPU):
    """Rule 7, once per run and box-wide: what a run left running in a scratch directory after the run itself
    went. The unit is the workspace — every process working in one belongs to the same run, so they get one
    verdict and, when it is `end`, they all go: the server, and the workers it had spawned. This process and its
    own ancestors are not holders (a sweep must not read itself as somebody still working here, nor end its own
    shell), and neither is another user's process, which this may not touch and does not own.

    Nothing is removed from disk here: rule 8 is the pass that clears what the runs left behind, and this one
    ends only what is still running."""
    if hours <= 0:
        return
    tmp = os.path.realpath(tempfile.gettempdir())
    mine = own_chain()
    holders = {}
    for pid, cwd in r.holds.cwds:
        ws = scratch(cwd, tmp)
        pid = int(pid) if str(pid).isdigit() else 0
        if ws is None or pid in mine or not ours(pid):
            continue
        st = proc_stat(pid)
        if st is None or st[2] <= 0:                    # no readable start time is no evidence of age, so no candidate
            continue
        holders.setdefault(ws, []).append((pid, st))
    hits = []
    for ws in sorted(holders):
        pids = holders[ws]
        ages = [max(1.0, now() - st[2]) for _, st in pids]
        # A session leader that still exists is a run still going — whoever adopted these processes when their
        # parent died. One that made its own session (`setsid`, a service) is its own leader, so it is never an orphan.
        run = next((f"pid {st[1]} ({pcomm(st[1])})" for _, st in pids if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{st[1]}")), "")
        f = {"ws": ws, "age": max(ages), "cpu": max(st[3] / a for (_, st), a in zip(pids, ages)),
             "run": run, "held": r.holds.records_on(ws), "hours": hours, "share": share}
        verdict, why = orphan_verdict(f)
        if verdict != "keep":
            hits.append((ws, pids, verdict, why))
    if not hits:
        return
    print(f"cc-janitor scratch workspaces{' (dry run)' if r.dry else ''}")
    for ws, pids, verdict, why in hits:
        if verdict == "report":
            r.report("scratch", ws, why)
            continue
        for pid, _ in sorted(pids):
            r.act_here("scratch", "end", f"pid {pid} ({pcomm(pid)})", why, lambda p=pid, w=ws: end_proc(p, w))


def sweep_leftovers(r, days):
    """Rule 8, once per run and box-wide: what finished runs LEFT ON DISK in the temp root. Every entry in it is
    measured — the last time anything in it was written, what it is holding, and its shape — and one this user
    owns, that is a plain tree, that nothing has written to for `days`, and that neither hold test holds, is
    removed whole. The root itself is never a candidate: the unit is what a run made in it, exactly as rule 7
    reads it.

    A busy box leaves tens of thousands of these, so what a person reads is one line — how many, how much room
    they were holding, and the largest of them. The spares are named one by one: that is the half worth reading,
    and the half this rule is judged on."""
    if days <= 0:
        return
    tmp = os.path.realpath(tempfile.gettempdir())
    try:
        dev = os.lstat(tmp).st_dev
        names = sorted(os.listdir(tmp))
    except OSError as e:
        r.report("scratch", tmp, f"the temp root could not be read: {e} — nothing was removed")
        return
    # The pass's own reading, taken before it started walking. CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_BLIND drops it — tests: the
    # second look below has to be reachable as the only thing standing between the sweep and what is in use.
    held = {} if os.environ.get("CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_BLIND") else r.holds.under(tmp)
    take, spared = [], []
    for name in names:
        p = f"{tmp}/{name}"
        f = scratch_scan(p, dev)
        if f is None:
            continue
        f.update(path=p, days=days, age=max(0.0, now() - f["newest"]), held=held.get(p, []))
        verdict, why = leftover_verdict(f)
        if verdict == "rm":
            take.append((p, f["bytes"]))
        elif verdict == "report":
            spared.append((p, why))
    if not take and not spared:
        return
    print(f"cc-janitor scratch leftovers{' (dry run)' if r.dry else ''}")
    for p, why in spared:
        r.report("scratch", p, why)
    if not take:
        return

    def summary(rows, freed):
        big = max(rows, key=lambda t: t[1])
        return (f"{size(freed)}, nothing written in them for {dur(days * 86400)} or more"
                f" · largest {big[0]} ({size(big[1])})")

    if r.dry:
        r.log("scratch", "rm", f"{len(take)} leftovers under {tmp}", summary(take, sum(b for _, b in take)))
        r.did += [p for p, _ in take]
        return
    # Look again, on readings taken NOW: this pass has been walking a temp root since it read /proc, and what it
    # removes it cannot get back. A candidate something has written to since, or that something is now working
    # in, fails its own rule the second time and stays — with the reason, whichever of the two it is.
    again = Holds().under(tmp)
    gone, freed, failed = [], 0, []
    for p, b in take:
        f = scratch_scan(p, dev)
        if f is None:
            continue                                    # gone already: this pass's job, done by somebody else
        f.update(path=p, days=days, age=max(0.0, now() - f["newest"]), held=again.get(p, []))
        verdict, why = leftover_verdict(f)
        if verdict != "rm":
            r.report("scratch", p, why or f"spared {p}: it changed while this pass was reading the temp root")
            continue
        err = remove_path(p)
        if err:
            failed.append((p, err))
            continue
        gone.append((p, b))
        freed += b
        r.did.append(p)
    for p, err in failed[:3]:
        r.report("scratch", p, f"rm failed: {err}")
    if len(failed) > 3:
        r.report("scratch", tmp, f"+{len(failed) - 3} more leftovers could not be removed")
    if gone:
        r.log("scratch", "rm", f"{len(gone)} leftovers under {tmp}", summary(gone, freed))


def fixtures():
    """{pid: [paths]} of every `_cc<suite><pid>` thing on this box: the board and its lock, worktrees, state, the ~/dev
    clone, the run's scratch dir, its entry in the deploy ledger, its context dir — the namespace a suite gives one run
    (RUN=$$) and its own trap removes, when the run lives to reach the trap. The glob is the prefix, so it also reaches
    names with no pid in them (`_cclive.lock`); the pid below is what decides, and those match nothing and are left."""
    found = {}
    for pat in (f"{BOARDS}/{FIXTURE}*", f"{WTS}/{FIXTURE}*", f"{STATE}/{FIXTURE}*", f"{DEV}/{FIXTURE}*",
                f"{HOME}/.cc/selftest-[0-9]*", f"{STATE}/land/{FIXTURE}*.applied", f"{HOME}/.claude/projects/{FIXTURE}*"):
        for p in glob.glob(pat):
            m = re.search(r"(?:_cc[a-z]+|selftest-)(\d+)", os.path.basename(p))
            if m:
                found.setdefault(int(m.group(1)), []).append(p)
    return found


def fixture_pid(name):
    """The suite pid a fixture's name STARTS with (`_cctest<pid>`, `_ccsbx<pid>/t`, `_cctest<pid>/hx~next`), or None."""
    m = SUITE.match(name)
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else None


def line_pid(line):
    """…and the one a handoff-ledger line carries anywhere in it, or None. Searched rather than sliced at the first
    `_cc`: the prefix alone is short enough to hit some other word first and lose the fixture that follows it."""
    m = SUITE.search(line)
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else None


def sweep_fixtures(r):
    """Rule 6, box-wide: what a test run left behind. Every fixture a suite makes carries the run's pid, and a suite
    that died mid-run (a kill, a usage limit, a restart) never reached its own trap: a `_cctest<pid>` board whose
    `waiting` row read like a real ask and reached the owner's NEEDS YOU list, a phantom repo in the deploy ledger on
    every catch-up tick, lines in the handoff ledger the Home tab tails (2026-09-01), a `_ccsbx<pid>` repo whose two
    worktrees the board watchdog reported as work no board knew (2026-09-02). THE PID IS THE WHOLE TEST: alive →
    the suite is running and everything it made is its (a reused pid keeps a leftover a while longer, which is the cheap
    direction); gone → removed, and the ledger loses its lines. cc-reconcile's 20-min pass calls `--fixtures` for this
    alone; the daily run does it with the rest."""
    found = fixtures()
    wins = [(wid, name, fixture_pid(name)) for wid, name in windows() if fixture_pid(name) is not None]
    for _, _, pid in wins:
        found.setdefault(pid, [])
    ledger = f"{STATE}/context/handoff.log"
    try:
        lines = open(ledger, errors="replace").read().splitlines(keepends=True)
    except OSError:
        lines = []
    for ln in lines:
        pid = line_pid(ln)
        if pid is not None:
            found.setdefault(pid, [])
    dead = sorted(pid for pid in found if not os.path.exists(f"/proc/{pid}"))
    if not dead:
        return
    print(f"cc-janitor selftest fixtures{' (dry run)' if r.dry else ''}")
    swept = []
    for pid in dead:
        why = f"left by selftest run {pid}, which is gone"
        # The pid says WHOSE a fixture is, not whether anything is still in it. One held path holds the whole
        # run: taking the board row, the state dir and the clone out from under a live process is the same
        # accident as taking its worktree, and killing its window would kill what this just spared.
        spared = {}
        for p in sorted(found[pid]):
            for s in r.holds.on(p):
                spared.setdefault(s, p)
        if spared:
            r.report("selftest", f"selftest run {pid}",
                     "spared " + ", ".join(sorted(set(spared.values()))) + ": " + "; ".join(spared))
            continue
        swept.append(pid)
        for p in sorted(found[pid]):
            r.act("selftest", "rm", p, why, ["rm", "-rf", "--", p])
        for wid, name, wpid in wins:
            if wpid == pid:
                r.act("selftest", "kill window", f"{name} ({wid})", why, ["tmux", "kill-window", "-t", wid])
    keep = [ln for ln in lines if line_pid(ln) not in swept]
    if len(keep) < len(lines):
        why = f"{len(lines) - len(keep)} lines of selftest runs that are gone"
        if not r.dry:
            try:
                with open(ledger + ".tmp", "w") as f:
                    f.writelines(keep)
                os.replace(ledger + ".tmp", ledger)
            except OSError as e:
                r.report("selftest", ledger, f"scrub failed: {e}")
                return
        r.log("selftest", "scrub", ledger, why)
        r.did.append(ledger)


def setting(key, default=""):
    """A ~/.cc/config value through cc-config, the file's one reader: the environment wins, set-but-empty included."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-config", "get", key, default], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
        return p.stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        return os.environ.get(key, default)


def configured():
    """The repos the timer sweeps: CC_JANITOR_REPOS in ~/.cc/config, whitespace separated. Unset means this box
    has not pointed the janitor at any repo yet, and a janitor with no target does nothing rather than guess."""
    return setting("CC_JANITOR_REPOS").split()


def container_days():
    """Rule 5's threshold in days: CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS, default 7. 0 turns the container sweep off, and so does
    the key set but empty (`CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS= cc-janitor …`, the box-wide off idiom) — never int(''). A value
    that is not a number is the default — a typo must not become a one-day sweep."""
    v = setting("CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS", str(CONTAINER_DAYS)).strip()
    if not v:
        return 0
    try:
        return max(0, int(v))
    except ValueError:
        return CONTAINER_DAYS


def orphan_hours():
    """Rule 7's threshold in hours: CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_HOURS, default 4. 0 turns rule 7 off, and so does the key set
    but empty. A fraction is a fraction of an hour — which is how the selfcheck drives the rule against a process it
    started a moment ago. A value that is not a number is the default: a typo must not end everything in /tmp."""
    v = setting("CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_HOURS", str(ORPHAN_HOURS)).strip()
    if not v:
        return 0.0
    try:
        return max(0.0, float(v))
    except ValueError:
        return float(ORPHAN_HOURS)


def orphan_cpu():
    """…and the share of a core below which rule 7 reports an orphan instead of ending it: CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_CPU,
    default 0.10. Unreadable or unset is the default — never 0, which would end every idle leftover on the box."""
    try:
        return max(0.0, float(setting("CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_CPU", str(ORPHAN_CPU)).strip()))
    except ValueError:
        return ORPHAN_CPU


def scratch_days():
    """Rule 8's threshold in days: CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS, default 7. 0 turns the rule off, and so does the key
    set but empty. A fraction is a fraction of a day — which is how the selfcheck drives the rule against a
    directory it made a moment ago. A value that is not a number is the default: a typo must not take everything
    written in the temp root today."""
    v = setting("CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS", str(SCRATCH_DAYS)).strip()
    if not v:
        return 0.0
    try:
        return max(0.0, float(v))
    except ValueError:
        return float(SCRATCH_DAYS)


def main(argv):
    if argv[:1] == ["selfcheck"]:
        return selfcheck()
    dry = "--dry" in argv
    net = "--no-net" not in argv
    only_fixtures = "--fixtures" in argv                # rule 6 alone: cheap enough for cc-reconcile's 20-min pass
    repos = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    if "--configured" in argv:
        repos = configured()
        if not repos:                                   # rule 5 still runs: a container is pointed by its name
            print("cc-janitor: no repos configured (CC_JANITOR_REPOS in ~/.cc/config) — no repo swept")
    elif not repos and not only_fixtures:
        print(__doc__.strip().splitlines()[2], file=sys.stderr)
        print("usage: cc-janitor <repo> [<repo> …] [--dry] | --configured [--dry] | --fixtures [--dry] | selfcheck", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    r = Run(dry)
    if not only_fixtures:
        for repo in repos:
            if "/" in repo or repo.startswith("."):
                print(f"cc-janitor: '{repo}' is not a repo name", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            sweep(repo, r, net)
        sweep_containers(r, container_days())
        sweep_orphans(r, orphan_hours(), orphan_cpu())
        sweep_leftovers(r, scratch_days())
    sweep_fixtures(r)
    print(f"cc-janitor: {len(r.did)} swept, {len(r.said)} reported"
          f"{' (dry run — nothing changed)' if dry else f' · log {LOG}'}")
    return 0


# ── selfcheck ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def selfcheck():
    """The decision table, then one end-to-end sweep of a fake box: its own HOME, its own board, its own
    worktrees, a bare repo standing in for origin, and stub `tmux`/`gh` on PATH. Nothing here touches this
    box's tmux server, its boards or GitHub, its temp root or anything running in it, and every fixture name is a
    placeholder."""
    p = f = 0

    def ok(name, cond):
        nonlocal p, f
        if cond:
            p += 1
        else:
            f += 1
            print(f"  FAIL {name}")

    # rule 1 — a terminal track, and the four things that stop it being swept
    def T(**kw):
        d = {"status": "merged", "age": 5 * 86400, "dirty": False, "snap": None}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a track merged for days is removed", track_verdict(T())[0] == "rm")
    ok("…and `done` is swept exactly like `merged`", track_verdict(T(status="done"))[0] == "rm")
    ok("a track merged an hour ago is left alone — and says nothing, because young is not a finding",
       track_verdict(T(age=3600)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("UNCOMMITTED WORK SURVIVES: the worktree is reported, never removed",
       track_verdict(T(dirty=True))[0] == "report" and "uncommitted" in track_verdict(T(dirty=True))[1])
    ok("a track cc-reconcile still calls live is reported, whatever the board says",
       track_verdict(T(snap={"live": True, "state": "merged"}))[0] == "report")
    ok("…and so is one the snapshot and the board disagree about",
       track_verdict(T(snap={"live": False, "state": "running"}))[0] == "report")
    ok("a snapshot that agrees is no obstacle", track_verdict(T(snap={"live": False, "state": "merged"}))[0] == "rm")
    for st in ("queued", "running", "waiting", "review", "blocked"):
        ok(f"rule 4: a {st} track is never touched", track_verdict(T(status=st)) == ("keep", ""))

    # rule 2 — the remote branch
    def B(**kw):
        d = {"board": "", "pr": None, "dirty": False}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a branch whose PR merged is deleted on origin", branch_verdict(B(pr="MERGED"))[0] == "delete")
    ok("a branch with NO PR is reported, never deleted", branch_verdict(B())[0] == "report")
    ok("an open PR's branch is reported", branch_verdict(B(pr="OPEN"))[0] == "report")
    ok("a PR that CLOSED without merging is reported — the one case a guess would cost real work",
       branch_verdict(B(pr="CLOSED"))[0] == "report")
    ok("a PR state that could not be read is reported, not acted on", branch_verdict(B(pr="?"))[0] == "report")
    ok("rule 4: a merged PR does not free a branch whose track is still running",
       branch_verdict(B(board="running", pr="MERGED")) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("…nor one whose worktree has uncommitted changes",
       branch_verdict(B(pr="MERGED", dirty=True))[0] == "report")
    ok("a merged PR on a track already merged on the board is deleted",
       branch_verdict(B(board="merged", pr="MERGED"))[0] == "delete")

    # rules 3 and 4 — the tmux window
    def W(**kw):
        d = {"board": "", "bare": True}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a bare shell on a track the board never heard of is killed", window_verdict(W())[0] == "kill")
    ok("…and on a merged track too", window_verdict(W(board="merged"))[0] == "kill")
    ok("A LIVE WINDOW SURVIVES: it is reported, never killed", window_verdict(W(bare=False))[0] == "report")
    ok("rule 4: a window whose track is still running is left alone and unremarked",
       window_verdict(W(board="running")) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("…for every non-terminal word the board has",
       all(window_verdict(W(board=s)) == ("keep", "") for s in ("queued", "waiting", "review", "blocked")))

    # rule 5 — the ccbox container
    def C(**kw):
        d = {"name": "ccbox-sample", "status": "exited", "age": 10 * 86400, "days": 7}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a ccbox container stopped for over a week is removed", container_verdict(C())[0] == "rm")
    ok("…one stopped two days ago is left alone, and unremarked", container_verdict(C(age=2 * 86400)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("…and the week is the configured one", container_verdict(C(age=2 * 86400, days=1))[0] == "rm")
    ok("A RUNNING CONTAINER IS NEVER A CANDIDATE, whatever its last stop says",
       container_verdict(C(status="running")) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("only ccbox's own, by name — the rest of the box's docker is not this tool's business",
       container_verdict(C(name="other-sample", age=90 * 86400)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("a stop time that cannot be read holds the sweep and is reported — unknown is not 'long ago'",
       container_verdict(C(age=None))[0] == "report")
    ok("docker's FinishedAt reads, nanoseconds and all — and nonsense reads as None, never as 0",
       stopped_at("2026-08-30T01:44:43.257081924Z") == epoch("2026-08-30T01:44:43Z") and stopped_at("not-a-time") is None)

    # rule 7 — a process still working in a scratch workspace after the run that made it has gone
    def O(**kw):
        d = {"ws": "/tmp/ws", "age": 10 * 3600, "cpu": 0.5, "run": "", "held": [], "hours": 4, "share": 0.10}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a process burning a core in a scratch workspace whose run is gone is ended", orphan_verdict(O())[0] == "end")
    ok("…and the pass says where it was working and what it was costing",
       "/tmp/ws" in orphan_verdict(O())[1] and "50% of a core" in orphan_verdict(O())[1])
    ok("one an hour old is a run in progress, left alone and unremarked", orphan_verdict(O(age=3600)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("…and the hours are the configured ones", orphan_verdict(O(age=3600, hours=0.5))[0] == "end")
    ok("A RUN STILL GOING SURVIVES: a session leader that is still there is reported, never ended",
       orphan_verdict(O(run="pid 42 (bash)"))[0] == "report" and "still alive" in orphan_verdict(O(run="pid 42 (bash)"))[1])
    ok("AN IDLE ORPHAN SURVIVES — a preview sitting on a port costs nobody anything, so it is a finding, not a kill",
       orphan_verdict(O(cpu=0.001))[0] == "report" and "idle" in orphan_verdict(O(cpu=0.001))[1])
    ok("…and that share is the configured one too", orphan_verdict(O(cpu=0.05, share=0.01))[0] == "end")
    ok("A WORKSPACE A REGISTERED RECORD POINTS INTO SURVIVES, however hard it is running, and the record is named",
       orphan_verdict(O(held=["/x/rec.json points into it"], cpu=9.0))[0] == "report"
       and "/x/rec.json" in orphan_verdict(O(held=["/x/rec.json points into it"]))[1])
    ok("a scratch workspace is the ONE directory below the temp root — not the root, and nothing outside it",
       scratch("/tmp/ws/a/b", "/tmp") == "/tmp/ws" and scratch("/tmp/ws", "/tmp") == "/tmp/ws"
       and scratch("/tmp", "/tmp") is None and scratch("/home/x", "/tmp") is None and scratch("/tmpx/y", "/tmp") is None)
    ok("a process's own /proc row reads: its session, when it started, and the CPU it has burned since",
       (lambda s: bool(s) and s[1] > 0 and 0 <= now() - s[2] < 10 * 365 * 86400 and s[3] >= 0)(proc_stat(os.getpid())))
    ok("…and THIS process and everything above it is never a candidate, wherever it happens to be working",
       os.getpid() in own_chain() and 1 in own_chain() and proc_stat(-1) is None)

    # rule 8 — what a finished run left on disk in the temp root
    def L(**kw):
        d = {"path": "/tmp/ws", "ours": True, "plain": True, "age": 30 * 86400, "held": [], "days": 7}
        d.update(kw)
        return d

    ok("a scratch entry nothing has written in for weeks is removed", leftover_verdict(L())[0] == "rm")
    ok("…one written in yesterday is left alone, and unremarked", leftover_verdict(L(age=86400)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("…and the days are the configured ones", leftover_verdict(L(age=86400, days=0.5))[0] == "rm")
    ok("A DIRECTORY SOMETHING IS WORKING IN SURVIVES, however long its own clock has stood still, and what held "
       "it is named", leftover_verdict(L(held=["pid 42 (node) is working in it"]))[0] == "report"
       and "pid 42 (node)" in leftover_verdict(L(held=["pid 42 (node) is working in it"]))[1])
    ok("…and so does one a REGISTERED RECORD points into",
       leftover_verdict(L(held=["/x/rec.json points into it"]))[0] == "report"
       and "/x/rec.json" in leftover_verdict(L(held=["/x/rec.json points into it"]))[1])
    ok("ANOTHER USER'S FILE IS NOT THIS SWEEP'S TO TAKE, at any age",
       leftover_verdict(L(ours=False)) == ("keep", "") and leftover_verdict(L(ours=False, age=10 ** 9)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("A DOOR IS NOT A LEFTOVER: a tree holding a socket, a fifo, a device or a mount is never a candidate, "
       "whatever its age", leftover_verdict(L(plain=False)) == ("keep", "")
       and leftover_verdict(L(plain=False, age=10 ** 9)) == ("keep", ""))
    ok("what a sweep freed is said in units a person reads",
       size(512) == "512 B" and size(5 << 20) == "5.0 MB" and size(3 << 30) == "3.0 GB")

    # the hold — what counts as being inside the directory a rule is about to remove
    ok("a directory is held by its own path and by anything under it, and by nothing beside it",
       inside("/a/b", "/a/b") and inside("/a/b", "/a/b/c/d") and inside("/a/b/", "/a/b/c")
       and not inside("/a/b", "/a/bb") and not inside("/a/b", "/a") and not inside("/a/b", "/x/a/b"))

    # end to end: a fake box of its own — board, worktrees, origin, tmux and gh all stubbed
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as th:
        repo = "sampleproj"
        fake, dev, wts = f"{th}/fakebin", f"{th}/dev/{repo}", f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{repo}"
        os.makedirs(fake)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/state", exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/tmp", exist_ok=True)
        ftmp = os.path.realpath(f"{th}/tmp")    # rule 7's temp root, for EVERY run below: no pass here sees the real one
        env = dict(os.environ, HOME=th, TMPDIR=ftmp, PATH=f"{fake}:{os.environ.get('PATH', '')}",
                   CC_FAKE_TMUX=f"{th}/tmux.txt", CC_FAKE_GH=f"{th}/gh.json", CC_FAKE_DOCKER=f"{th}/docker.txt",
                   CC_JANITOR_HOLDS="~/.cc/state/records/*.json",
                   GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="t", GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="t@t", GIT_COMMITTER_NAME="t", GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL="t@t")
        for name, body in (("tmux", FAKE_TMUX), ("gh", FAKE_GH), ("docker", FAKE_DOCKER)):
            open(f"{fake}/{name}", "w").write(body)
            os.chmod(f"{fake}/{name}", 0o755)

        def sh(*a, **kw):
            return subprocess.run(a, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120, **kw)

        # a real repo with a real "origin", so a branch deletion is a real branch deletion
        sh("git", "init", "-q", "-b", "main", dev)
        open(f"{dev}/README", "w").write("x\n")
        sh("git", "-C", dev, "add", "-A")
        sh("git", "-C", dev, "commit", "-qm", "init")
        sh("git", "init", "-q", "--bare", f"{th}/origin.git")
        sh("git", "-C", dev, "remote", "add", "origin", f"{th}/origin.git")
        sh("git", "-C", dev, "push", "-q", "origin", "main")
        for b in ("gone", "openpr", "nopr"):
            sh("git", "-C", dev, "push", "-q", "origin", f"main:refs/heads/track/{b}")

        def cb(*a):
            return subprocess.run([f"{BIN}/cc-board", *a], env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)

        cb("init", repo, dev, "main")
        for track, st in (("gone", "merged"), ("dirtyone", "merged"), ("young", "merged"), ("busy", "running"),
                          ("served", "merged"), ("listed", "merged"), ("staleref", "merged")):
            cb("add", repo, track, track, "")
            cb("status", repo, track, st)
            os.makedirs(f"{wts}/{track}", exist_ok=True)
        # one worktree with work nobody committed: it must come out the far side untouched
        sh("git", "-C", dev, "worktree", "add", "-q", "-b", "track/dirtyone", f"{wts}/dirtyone", "main")
        open(f"{wts}/dirtyone/scratch.txt", "w").write("not committed\n")
        # backdate every merged track past the 48 h line except `young`, by rewriting the log the board keeps
        bj = f"{th}/.cc/boards/{repo}.json"
        d = board_read(bj)
        old = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(time.time() - 5 * 86400))
        for e in d["log"]:
            if e.get("track") in ("gone", "dirtyone", "served", "listed", "staleref") \
                    and str(e.get("msg", "")).startswith("status -> "):
                e["ts"] = old
        json.dump(d, open(bj, "w"), indent=2)
        # A PIPE PLANTED WHERE A BOARD SHOULD BE (review of #224): the read is cc-board's opener now, so it is no board, never a hang
        def bounded(fn, secs=10):   # a read that hangs on the pipe (a plain open would, for good) fails THIS case, not the suite
            import threading
            box = []
            def go():
                try: box.append(fn())
                except Exception as e: box.append(e)
            t = threading.Thread(target=go, daemon=True); t.start(); t.join(secs)
            return box[0] if box else "hung"
        global BOARDS
        _b, BOARDS = BOARDS, f"{th}/.cc/boards"
        os.mkfifo(f"{BOARDS}/pipe.json")
        ok("a pipe planted where a board should be reads as no board, not a hang, and the real one still reads",
           bounded(lambda: board("pipe")) is None and board(repo) is not None)
        os.remove(f"{BOARDS}/pipe.json"); BOARDS = _b

        # what a box registers as "something of mine is in there": JSON with a path in it. One fresh, one
        # nobody has rewritten in days (which is not evidence), one that is not JSON at all (which is none either)
        recs = f"{th}/.cc/state/records"
        os.makedirs(recs, exist_ok=True)
        json.dump({"name": "one", "dir": f"{wts}/listed", "port": 1}, open(f"{recs}/one.json", "w"))
        json.dump({"name": "old", "dir": f"{wts}/staleref"}, open(f"{recs}/old.json", "w"))
        os.utime(f"{recs}/old.json", (now() - 5 * 86400,) * 2)
        open(f"{recs}/broken.json", "w").write("{not json")

        # two panes: one with a child (live) and one without (bare) — the same fact `cc` reads off a real pane
        # Groups of their own, with no handle on this process's stdout: a stray `sleep` holding the pipe open
        # keeps a `… | tail` waiting for it, which is two silent minutes at the end of the suite.
        def fixture(*cmd, **kw):
            return subprocess.Popen(cmd, start_new_session=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                    stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, **kw)

        live = fixture("bash", "-c", "sleep 120; true")  # `; true`, or bash execs sleep in its own place and has no child
        bare = fixture("sleep", "120")
        served = fixture("sleep", "300", cwd=f"{wts}/served")   # …and one standing in a worktree, as a dev server does
        time.sleep(0.4)                                 # the live pane's shell needs its child to exist first
        tmuxf = f"{th}/tmux.txt"

        def wins():
            return [ln.split("\t")[1] for ln in open(tmuxf).read().splitlines() if ln.strip()]

        def setwins():
            open(tmuxf, "w").write(
                f"@1\t{repo}\t{bare.pid}\n"                    # the planning window: bare, and never a candidate
                f"@2\t{repo}/ghost\t{bare.pid}\n"              # no board row, nothing running → killed
                f"@3\t{repo}/busy\t{bare.pid}\n"               # rule 4: its track is running
                f"@4\t{repo}/held{NEXT}\t{live.pid}\n"         # a handoff leftover with a live process → reported
                f"@5\t{repo}/stalenext{NEXT}\t{bare.pid}\n")   # …and one without → killed, like its base track
        setwins()
        json.dump([{"number": 1, "state": "MERGED", "headRefName": "track/gone"},
                   {"number": 2, "state": "OPEN", "headRefName": "track/openpr"}], open(f"{th}/gh.json", "w"))
        # five containers on the fake docker, one of them rule 5's
        dockf = f"{th}/docker.txt"

        def stamp(days):
            return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.123456789Z", time.gmtime(time.time() - days * 86400))

        rows = (f"ccbox-old\texited\t{stamp(10)}\n"          # stopped 10 days ago → removed
                f"ccbox-fresh\texited\t{stamp(2)}\n"         # stopped 2 days ago → left alone, unremarked
                f"ccbox-live\trunning\t{stamp(30)}\n"        # running: never a candidate, whatever its last stop says
                f"other-old\texited\t{stamp(30)}\n"          # not ccbox's → not this tool's business
                f"ccbox-odd\texited\tnot-a-time\n")          # a stop time nobody can read → reported, kept
        open(dockf, "w").write(rows)

        def run(*a, **extra):
            return subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo, *a],
                                  env=dict(env, **extra), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)

        # --dry first, against the very same fixture the real run then gets
        before = (sorted(board_read(bj)["tracks"]), sorted(wins()),
                  sh("git", "-C", dev, "ls-remote", "--heads", "origin").stdout)
        rd = run("--dry")
        ok("--dry says what it would do", "would rm" in rd.stdout and "would delete" in rd.stdout)
        ok("--DRY CHANGES NOTHING — board, windows and origin are all exactly as they were",
           (sorted(board_read(bj)["tracks"]), sorted(wins()),
            sh("git", "-C", dev, "ls-remote", "--heads", "origin").stdout) == before)
        ok("--dry writes no log either, nor anyone else's file — it refreshes no snapshot to get its answer",
           not os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor.log") and not os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/state/reconcile.json")
           and not os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor-spares.json"))
        ok("…and says so, rather than let a preview look surer about what is alive than the real pass is",
           "snapshot is stale" in rd.stdout)
        ok("--dry previews the container sweep too, and removes none",
           "would rm container ccbox-old" in rd.stdout and open(dockf).read() == rows)
        ok("CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS moves the line — 1 day would take the fresh one too",
           "would rm container ccbox-fresh" in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS="1").stdout)
        ok("…and 0 turns rule 5 off", "container" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS="0").stdout)
        ok("…and so does the key set but empty — off, not int('') and not the 7-day default",
           "container" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_CONTAINER_DAYS="").stdout)
        ok("a docker that cannot be asked is reported, and nothing is touched",
           "docker could not be asked" in run("--dry", CC_FAKE_DOCKER_DOWN="1").stdout and open(dockf).read() == rows)

        rn = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo],
                            env=dict(env, CC_JANITOR_NO_SNAP="1"), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)
        ok("no liveness snapshot: the deletion rules HOLD — report-only, nothing removed",
           "gone" in board_read(bj)["tracks"] and "held: no fresh liveness snapshot" in rn.stdout)

        r1 = run()
        b2 = board_read(bj)["tracks"]
        ok("end to end: the track merged 5 days ago is gone — worktree, branch, board row and all",
           "gone" not in b2 and not os.path.isdir(f"{wts}/gone"))
        ok("end to end: UNCOMMITTED WORK SURVIVES the sweep", "dirtyone" in b2 and os.path.exists(f"{wts}/dirtyone/scratch.txt"))
        ok("end to end: …and is reported so somebody deals with it", "uncommitted" in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: a track merged minutes ago is still there", "young" in b2)
        ok("end to end: rule 4 — the running track is untouched", "busy" in b2)
        ok("end to end: A WORKTREE A LIVE PROCESS IS WORKING IN SURVIVES — and the sweep says it spared it, and what held it",
           "served" in b2 and os.path.isdir(f"{wts}/served")
           and f"spared {wts}/served" in r1.stdout and "is working in it" in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: …and so does one a registered record points into, named by the record that holds it",
           "listed" in b2 and os.path.isdir(f"{wts}/listed") and f"{recs}/one.json points into it" in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: …but a record nobody has rewritten in days holds nothing, and one that is not JSON is not an "
           "obstacle either — a worktree with neither goes, exactly as before",
           "staleref" not in b2 and not os.path.isdir(f"{wts}/staleref"))
        remote = sh("git", "-C", dev, "ls-remote", "--heads", "origin").stdout
        ok("end to end: the merged PR's branch is gone from origin", "refs/heads/track/gone" not in remote)
        ok("end to end: the open PR's branch is still there, and reported",
           "refs/heads/track/openpr" in remote and "origin/track/openpr" in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: a branch with no PR at all is still there, and reported",
           "refs/heads/track/nopr" in remote and "no PR was ever opened" in r1.stdout)
        w = wins()
        ok("end to end: the bare window for a track the board never had is killed", f"{repo}/ghost" not in w)
        ok("end to end: THE PLANNING WINDOW SURVIVES — it is named for the repo, not for a track", repo in w)
        ok("end to end: A LIVE WINDOW SURVIVES, and is reported", f"{repo}/held{NEXT}" in w and "live process" in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: a bare `~next` leftover follows its base track and is killed", f"{repo}/stalenext{NEXT}" not in w)
        ok("end to end: rule 4 — the running track's window survives", f"{repo}/busy" in w)
        dk = open(dockf).read()
        ok("end to end: the ccbox container stopped 10 days ago is removed — by the no-snapshot pass already, since\n"
           "           docker's own status is a container's liveness and rule 5 waits for no cc-reconcile snapshot",
           "ccbox-old" not in dk and "rm container ccbox-old" in rn.stdout)
        ok("end to end: a ccbox stopped 2 days ago, a running one and a container that is not ccbox's all survive",
           all(n in dk for n in ("ccbox-fresh", "ccbox-live", "other-old")) and "ccbox-fresh" not in r1.stdout)
        ok("end to end: a container whose stop time cannot be read is kept, and reported",
           "ccbox-odd" in dk and "container ccbox-odd" in r1.stdout)
        log = open(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor.log").read()
        ok("end to end: every action is in the log, with what it was and why",
           all(s in log for s in ("rm sampleproj/gone", "delete origin/track/gone", "kill window sampleproj/ghost",
                                  "rm container ccbox-old"))
           and "merged for 5d" in log)
        ok("end to end: it exits 0 — a sweep with findings is the tool working", r1.returncode == 0)

        setwins()
        r2 = run()
        ok("end to end: a second sweep finds nothing new to remove and still exits 0",
           r2.returncode == 0 and "rm sampleproj" not in r2.stdout and "rm container" not in r2.stdout)
        ok("end to end: …and it did not come back for the branch it already deleted",
           "delete origin/track/gone" not in r2.stdout)

        setwins()
        r3 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), repo],
                            env={k: v for k, v in env.items() if k != "CC_JANITOR_HOLDS"},
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)
        b3 = board_read(bj)["tracks"]
        ok("end to end: THE HOLD IS THE REGISTRATION — with the record no longer registered its worktree goes on the "
           "next pass, while the one with a live process in it is spared again",
           "listed" not in b3 and not os.path.isdir(f"{wts}/listed")
           and "served" in b3 and f"spared {wts}/served" in r3.stdout)
        ok("end to end: …and three passes over the same held worktree put ONE line in the log — a spare is a "
           "standing state, and the log is what happened",
           open(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor.log").read().count(f"spared {wts}/served") == 1)

        # a repo with NO origin at all. It is reported — unless it is a member workspace, which has none by design
        # (docs/design-member-workspaces.md) and was reported for it on every Sunday run.
        mem = "memberws"
        os.makedirs(f"{th}/dev/{mem}/.cc", exist_ok=True)
        sh("git", "init", "-q", "-b", "main", f"{th}/dev/{mem}")
        cb("init", mem, f"{th}/dev/{mem}", "main")
        rm1 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), mem, "--dry"], env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        open(f"{th}/dev/{mem}/.cc/member-workspace", "w").close()
        rm2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), mem, "--dry"], env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        ok("an ordinary repo whose origin cannot be listed is still reported",
           "origin could not be listed" in rm1.stdout)
        ok("…but a member workspace has no origin by design, so the same silence there is not a finding",
           "origin could not be listed" not in rm2.stdout and rm2.returncode == 0)

        # a janitor with no target does nothing rather than guess
        rc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--configured"],
                            env=dict(env, CC_JANITOR_REPOS=""), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("--configured with nothing configured sweeps nothing, and says so",
           rc.returncode == 0 and "no repos configured" in rc.stdout)
        ok("…and reads the repo list off the config when it is there",
           configured.__doc__ is not None
           and subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--configured", "--dry"],
                              env=dict(env, CC_JANITOR_REPOS=repo), capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=300).stdout.startswith(f"cc-janitor {repo} (dry run)"))
        ru = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__)], env=env,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("no repo named is a usage error, never a sweep of everything", ru.returncode == 2)

        # rule 6: a selftest run's leftovers. Two fixtures, one whose suite is gone and one whose suite is alive (its
        # pid is ours): the dead one's board, lock, worktree, state, clone, scratch dir, ledger entry, tmux window and
        # handoff-ledger lines go; the live one's, and the real repo's line beside them, stay
        deadp, livep = 99999999, os.getpid()
        for pid in (deadp, livep):
            fx = f"_cctest{pid}"
            for d_ in (f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{fx}/w1", f"{th}/.cc/state/{fx}/w1", f"{th}/dev/{fx}",
                       f"{th}/.cc/selftest-{pid}", f"{th}/.cc/state/land", f"{th}/.cc/state/context"):
                os.makedirs(d_, exist_ok=True)
            cb("init", fx, f"{th}/dev/{fx}", "main")
            open(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{fx}.applied", "w").write("{}\n")
            open(f"{th}/tmux.txt", "a").write(f"@9{int(pid == livep)}\t{fx}/w1\t1\n")
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/context/handoff.log", "w").write(
            f"cc-handoff: _cctest{deadp}/hx — gone\ncc-handoff: _cctest{livep}/hx — live\ncc-handoff: {repo} — real\n")

        def left(pid):
            fx = f"_cctest{pid}"
            return [x for x in (f"{th}/.cc/boards/{fx}.json", f"{th}/.cc/boards/{fx}.lock", f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{fx}",
                                f"{th}/.cc/state/{fx}", f"{th}/dev/{fx}", f"{th}/.cc/selftest-{pid}",
                                f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{fx}.applied") if os.path.exists(x)]

        before = left(deadp)
        rd = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures", "--dry"], env=env,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("--fixtures --dry names what it would remove and removes nothing",
           rd.returncode == 0 and f"would rm {th}/.cc/boards/_cctest{deadp}.json" in rd.stdout and left(deadp) == before)
        rf = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures"], env=env,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        led = open(f"{th}/.cc/state/context/handoff.log").read()
        ok("--fixtures alone is a sweep, not a usage error, and a fixture whose suite is gone is removed whole: board, lock, "
           "worktree, state, clone, scratch dir, deploy-ledger entry, tmux window, handoff-ledger lines",
           rf.returncode == 0 and len(before) == 7 and left(deadp) == []
           and f"_cctest{deadp}/w1" not in open(f"{th}/tmux.txt").read() and f"_cctest{deadp}" not in led)
        ok("…and one whose suite is ALIVE is left whole, with the real repo's ledger line",
           len(left(livep)) == 7 and f"_cctest{livep}/w1" in open(f"{th}/tmux.txt").read()
           and f"_cctest{livep}/hx" in led and f"{repo} — real" in led)
        ok("…and a second pass finds nothing and says nothing",
           "selftest fixtures" not in subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures"], env=env,
                                                     capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60).stdout)

        # …AND THE PREFIX IS THE NAMESPACE, NOT ONE SUITE'S NAME. cc-sandbox's selfchecks name their scratch
        # `_ccsbx<pid>` — a ~/dev clone and worktrees, no board at all — and a leaked one reached the board watchdog
        # as two worktrees of work no board knew (2026-09-02). Its own fixtures, and all three answers: the dead
        # suite's goes, the live suite's stays, and a name under the prefix carrying NO pid is nobody's run to prove
        # dead, so it is never touched — the pid is the only evidence this rule accepts.
        for pid in (deadp, livep):
            for w_ in ("t", "u"):
                os.makedirs(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx{pid}/{w_}", exist_ok=True)
            os.makedirs(f"{th}/dev/_ccsbx{pid}", exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{th}/.cc/boards/_cclive.lock", "w").close()
        rs = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures"], env=env,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("a leaked `_ccsbx<pid>` — cc-sandbox's scratch, with no board of its own — is swept on the same rule as "
           "`_cctest<pid>`, while the live suite's copy and a prefixed name with no pid in it are left alone",
           rs.returncode == 0 and not os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx{deadp}")
           and not os.path.exists(f"{th}/dev/_ccsbx{deadp}")
           and os.path.isdir(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx{livep}/u") and os.path.isdir(f"{th}/dev/_ccsbx{livep}")
           and os.path.exists(f"{th}/.cc/boards/_cclive.lock"))
        # …AND THE PID SAYS WHOSE A FIXTURE IS, NOT WHETHER SOMETHING IS STANDING IN IT — and a hold is on the
        # RUN, not on the one path it landed on. A suite that is gone left all of this and a process is working
        # in one of its worktrees: not one path goes, nor its window, nor its ledger line. Sparing the worktree
        # while the board row, the state dir and the clone went would leave a live process with its bookkeeping
        # deleted, which is the accident this hold exists to stop, and killing the window would kill what the
        # same pass just spared. 99999998 is above any pid this kernel hands out, so it never lives.
        heldp, hfx = 99999998, "_cctest99999998"
        for d_ in (f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{hfx}/w1", f"{th}/.cc/state/{hfx}/w1", f"{th}/dev/{hfx}",
                   f"{th}/.cc/selftest-{heldp}"):
            os.makedirs(d_, exist_ok=True)
        cb("init", hfx, f"{th}/dev/{hfx}", "main")
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/land/{hfx}.applied", "w").write("{}\n")
        open(f"{th}/tmux.txt", "a").write(f"@98\t{hfx}/w1\t1\n")
        open(f"{th}/.cc/state/context/handoff.log", "a").write(f"cc-handoff: {hfx}/hx — held\n")
        sitter = fixture("sleep", "120", cwd=f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/{hfx}/w1")
        rh = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures"], env=env,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("A DEAD SUITE'S RUN IS SPARED WHOLE while a process is working in one of its worktrees — board, lock, "
           "state, clone, scratch dir, ledger entry, tmux window and handoff line all stay — and the pass names it",
           len(left(heldp)) == 7 and f"{hfx}/w1" in open(f"{th}/tmux.txt").read()
           and f"{hfx}/hx" in open(f"{th}/.cc/state/context/handoff.log").read()
           and f"pid {sitter.pid}" in rh.stdout and f"spared {th}/.cc/worktrees/{hfx}" in rh.stdout)
        os.killpg(os.getpgid(sitter.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
        sitter.wait(timeout=10)
        rh2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.realpath(__file__), "--fixtures"], env=env,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        ok("…and the whole run goes on the first pass after that process does, window and ledger line with it",
           rh2.returncode == 0 and left(heldp) == [] and f"{hfx}/w1" not in open(f"{th}/tmux.txt").read()
           and f"{hfx}/hx" not in open(f"{th}/.cc/state/context/handoff.log").read())

        shutil.rmtree(f"{th}/.cc/worktrees/_ccsbx{livep}", ignore_errors=True)
        shutil.rmtree(f"{th}/dev/_ccsbx{livep}", ignore_errors=True)
        os.remove(f"{th}/.cc/boards/_cclive.lock")

        # RULE 7: what a run left running in a scratch workspace after the run itself went. Four of them under this
        # test's own temp root, and the four answers: an orphan burning CPU, a run still going, an orphan a registered
        # record points into, and an idle one. The thresholds are a fraction of an hour and a hundredth of a core,
        # which is the same rule read against processes a second old.
        def orphan(ws, burn=False):
            """A run whose session is gone: a leader of its own that starts the work and exits — exactly what a suite
            leaves behind when it dies before its teardown. The work stays, its session leader does not. `nice`, so
            the one case that has to burn a core does not take one from the work running beside this suite."""
            os.makedirs(ws, exist_ok=True)
            job = "nice -n 19 bash -c 'while :; do :; done'" if burn else "sleep 300"
            subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", f"{job} & exit 0"], cwd=ws, start_new_session=True, timeout=60,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)

        wsgone, wslive, wsrec, wsidle = (f"{ftmp}/gone", f"{ftmp}/live", f"{ftmp}/rec", f"{ftmp}/idle")
        orphan(wsgone, burn=True)
        orphan(wsrec, burn=True)
        orphan(wsidle)
        os.makedirs(wslive, exist_ok=True)
        runner = fixture("bash", "-c", "sleep 300", cwd=wslive)     # its own session, and it stays: a run still going
        json.dump({"name": "served", "dir": wsrec, "port": 2}, open(f"{recs}/served.json", "w"))
        time.sleep(0.7)                                 # …which is older than the fraction of an hour below

        def holding(ws):
            return sorted(int(pid) for pid, c in live_cwds() if inside(os.path.realpath(ws), c))

        orph = dict(CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_HOURS="0.0001", CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_CPU="0.01")
        was = {w: holding(w) for w in (wsgone, wslive, wsrec, wsidle)}
        ro = run("--dry", **orph)
        ok("rule 7: --dry names the process it would end, and where it is working",
           f"would end pid {was[wsgone][0]}" in ro.stdout and wsgone in ro.stdout)
        ok("rule 7: --DRY ENDS NOTHING — every one of them is still running",
           {w: holding(w) for w in (wsgone, wslive, wsrec, wsidle)} == was and all(was.values()))
        r7 = run(**orph)
        ok("rule 7: an orphan burning a core in a scratch workspace is ended, and each process it ended is named",
           holding(wsgone) == [] and f"end pid {was[wsgone][0]}" in r7.stdout and wsgone in r7.stdout)
        ok("rule 7: A RUN STILL GOING SURVIVES — its session leader is alive, and the pass says which",
           holding(wslive) == was[wslive] and f"spared {wslive}" in r7.stdout and "still alive" in r7.stdout)
        ok("rule 7: AN ORPHAN A REGISTERED RECORD POINTS INTO SURVIVES, however hard it is running, named by the record",
           holding(wsrec) == was[wsrec] and f"{recs}/served.json points into it" in r7.stdout)
        ok("rule 7: AN IDLE ORPHAN SURVIVES and is reported — a preview sitting on a port costs nobody anything",
           holding(wsidle) == was[wsidle] and f"spared {wsidle}" in r7.stdout and "idle" in r7.stdout)
        ok("rule 7: the end is in the log too, with where it was working and what it was costing",
           f"end pid {was[wsgone][0]}" in open(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor.log").read())
        ok("rule 7: …and 0 hours turns the rule off — the same box, the same orphans, and not a word about them",
           "scratch workspace" in run("--dry", **orph).stdout
           and "scratch workspace" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_ORPHAN_HOURS="0").stdout)
        stray = fixture("sleep", "120", cwd=wsidle)
        time.sleep(0.3)
        ok("rule 7: the kill reads the working directory ONE more time first — a pid that has moved on since the "
           "pass read it, or been handed to something else, is not the one this ends",
           end_proc(stray.pid, f"{ftmp}/somewhere-else").startswith("it is working somewhere else")
           and os.path.exists(f"/proc/{stray.pid}")
           and end_proc(stray.pid, wsidle) == "" and stray.wait(timeout=10) == -signal.SIGTERM)

        for w in (wsgone, wslive, wsrec, wsidle):       # whatever the pass spared is this suite's to clean up
            for pid in holding(w):
                try:
                    os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                except OSError:
                    pass
        runner.wait(timeout=10)

        # RULE 8: what those runs left ON DISK. Six things under this test's own temp root, and every answer the
        # rule has — a leftover tree from a finished run, a leftover file, a tree whose own clock is a month old
        # but whose contents are not, one a live process is working in, one a registered record points into, and
        # a directory holding a socket: the shape a terminal multiplexer leaves in a temp root the day it starts
        # and never touches again, which an age test on its own would take out from under every session on the box.
        import socket
        left, leftfile, touched = f"{ftmp}/left", f"{ftmp}/leftfile", f"{ftmp}/touched"
        serving, declared, door = f"{ftmp}/serving", f"{ftmp}/declared", f"{ftmp}/door"
        names8 = ("declared", "door", "left", "leftfile", "link", "serving", "touched")
        for d_ in (f"{left}/deep", touched, serving, declared, door):
            os.makedirs(d_, exist_ok=True)
        open(f"{left}/deep/big.bin", "w").write("x" * 4096)
        os.symlink(dev, f"{ftmp}/link")                 # …pointing out of the temp root altogether
        os.symlink(f"{dev}/README", f"{left}/deep/out")  # …and one inside a tree that is about to go
        open(leftfile, "w").write("y" * 1024)
        open(f"{serving}/cache.txt", "w").write("a server's own scratch\n")
        open(f"{declared}/build.txt", "w").write("a build somebody declared\n")
        open(f"{touched}/fresh.txt", "w").write("written just now\n")   # …the one write that keeps `touched`
        sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
        sock.bind(f"{door}/socket")
        month = now() - 30 * 86400
        for p_ in (left, f"{left}/deep", f"{left}/deep/big.bin", leftfile, touched, serving, f"{serving}/cache.txt",
                   declared, f"{declared}/build.txt", door, f"{door}/socket"):
            os.utime(p_, (month, month))                # …the directory last, so its own clock reads a month
        for l_ in (f"{ftmp}/link", f"{left}/deep/out"):     # …the links by their own clocks, never their targets
            os.utime(l_, (month, month), follow_symlinks=False)
        sitter8 = fixture("sleep", "300", cwd=serving)
        json.dump({"name": "build", "dir": declared}, open(f"{recs}/build.json", "w"))
        time.sleep(0.4)

        def eight():
            return sorted(n for n in names8 if os.path.lexists(f"{ftmp}/{n}"))

        ok("the holds answer a whole temp root in one walk of the readings — a working directory below it names "
           "the one entry it holds, and nothing beside it",
           (lambda h: f"pid {sitter8.pid}" in " ".join(h.get(serving, [])) and door not in h)(Holds().under(ftmp)))
        ok("a scratch entry is measured whole: the newest mtime anywhere in it, what it holds, and its shape — "
           "and a socket inside is what makes a tree no candidate",
           scratch_scan(left, os.lstat(ftmp).st_dev)["bytes"] >= 4096
           and now() - scratch_scan(touched, os.lstat(ftmp).st_dev)["newest"] < 600
           and now() - os.lstat(touched).st_mtime > 20 * 86400
           and scratch_scan(door, os.lstat(ftmp).st_dev)["plain"] is False
           and scratch_scan(left, os.lstat(ftmp).st_dev)["plain"] is True)
        r8 = run("--dry")
        ok("rule 8: --dry says how much it would clear and what the largest of it is",
           f"would rm 2 leftovers under {ftmp}" in r8.stdout and f"largest {left}" in r8.stdout)
        ok("rule 8: --DRY TOUCHES NOTHING — all six are still there, contents and all",
           eight() == sorted(names8) and os.path.exists(f"{left}/deep/big.bin"))
        r8b = run()
        ok("rule 8: a leftover from a finished run is cleared — the whole tree, and the file beside it",
           not os.path.exists(left) and not os.path.exists(leftfile) and f"rm 2 leftovers under {ftmp}" in r8b.stdout)
        ok("rule 8: A TREE SOMETHING WROTE IN MINUTES AGO SURVIVES, however old the directory's own clock is",
           os.path.isdir(touched) and os.path.exists(f"{touched}/fresh.txt"))
        ok("rule 8: A DIRECTORY A LIVE PROCESS IS WORKING IN SURVIVES, and the pass says it spared it and names "
           "what held it", os.path.exists(f"{serving}/cache.txt")
           and f"spared {serving}" in r8b.stdout and f"pid {sitter8.pid}" in r8b.stdout)
        ok("rule 8: …and so does one a registered record points into, named by the record that holds it",
           os.path.exists(f"{declared}/build.txt") and f"{recs}/build.json points into it" in r8b.stdout)
        ok("rule 8: A SOCKET DIRECTORY SURVIVES — nothing has touched it for a month and it is still a door",
           os.path.exists(f"{door}/socket"))
        ok("rule 8: what it cleared is in the log, with the room it made",
           f"rm 2 leftovers under {ftmp}" in open(f"{th}/.cc/state/janitor.log").read())
        ok("rule 8: …and 0 days turns the rule off — the same temp root, and not a word about it",
           "scratch leftovers" in run("--dry").stdout
           and "scratch leftovers" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="0").stdout
           and "scratch leftovers" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="").stdout)
        ok("rule 8: …and a value that is not a number is the DEFAULT — neither off, which would let a temp root "
           "fill again quietly, nor a sweep of everything written today",
           f"leftovers under {ftmp}" in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="0.000001").stdout
           and f"leftovers under {ftmp}" not in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="nonsense").stdout
           and f"spared {serving}" in run("--dry", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="nonsense").stdout)
        # …AND WITH THE AGE LINE AT ZERO THE HOLD IS ALL THAT IS LEFT. Every entry in the root is a candidate
        # now, so what comes through this comes through on what is using it and on nothing else.
        rz = run(CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="0.000001")
        ok("rule 8: with the age line at zero the young tree goes — being young was the only thing keeping it",
           not os.path.exists(touched))
        ok("rule 8: …and THE HELD ONES AND THE DOOR ARE STILL THERE, on the hold and the shape alone",
           os.path.exists(f"{serving}/cache.txt") and os.path.exists(f"{declared}/build.txt")
           and os.path.exists(f"{door}/socket")
           and f"spared {serving}" in rz.stdout and f"spared {declared}" in rz.stdout)
        ok("rule 8: A SYMLINK IS NOT A TREE: one in the temp root is never a candidate at any age, and what a "
           "tree that WAS swept pointed at is still where it was",
           os.path.islink(f"{ftmp}/link") and os.path.isdir(dev) and os.path.exists(f"{dev}/README"))
        # THE READING IT DELETES ON IS TAKEN AT THE MOMENT IT DELETES. A pass that read /proc before it walked a
        # temp root holds a minutes-old picture by the time it removes anything, and a process that started in
        # the meantime would not be in it. Here the first reading is dropped altogether, so the hold that spares
        # these two is the one taken at the removal itself.
        rb = run(CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_DAYS="0.000001", CC_JANITOR_SCRATCH_BLIND="1")
        ok("rule 8: THE SECOND LOOK IS WHAT IT DELETES ON — with the pass's own reading dropped, the directory a "
           "live process is working in and the one a record points into both still survive, and are named",
           os.path.exists(f"{serving}/cache.txt") and os.path.exists(f"{declared}/build.txt")
           and f"spared {serving}" in rb.stdout and f"spared {declared}" in rb.stdout
           and f"pid {sitter8.pid}" in rb.stdout and f"{recs}/build.json points into it" in rb.stdout)
        sock.close()
        os.killpg(os.getpgid(sitter8.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
        sitter8.wait(timeout=10)

        for proc in (live, bare, served):               # the group, so the `sleep` under the live shell goes too
            try:
                os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
            proc.wait(timeout=10)
    print(f"cc-janitor selfcheck: {p} passed, {f} failed")
    return 1 if f else 0


FAKE_TMUX = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub: windows are lines "id<TAB>name<TAB>pane_pid" in $CC_FAKE_TMUX. Speaks only the four verbs
# cc and cc-janitor use, and kill-window really removes the row so the test can see it happen.
set -u; F="$CC_FAKE_TMUX"; cmd="${1:-}"; shift || true
fmt=""; targ=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do case "$1" in -F) fmt="${2:-}"; shift 2;; -t) targ="${2:-}"; shift 2;; *) shift;; esac; done
case "$cmd" in
  has-session) exit 0;;
  list-windows|list-panes)
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r id name pid; do
      [ -n "${id:-}" ] || continue
      case "$targ" in ""|main) ;; *) [ "$targ" = "$id" ] || continue;; esac
      s="$fmt"; s="${s//\#\{window_id\}/$id}"; s="${s//\#\{window_name\}/$name}"
      s="${s//\#\{pane_pid\}/$pid}"; s="${s//\#\{pane_current_command\}/bash}"
      s="${s//\#W/$name}"; s="${s//\#I/${id#@}}"
      printf '%s\n' "$s"
    done < "$F";;
  kill-window)
    t=$(mktemp); awk -F'\t' -v k="$targ" '$1!=k' "$F" > "$t"; mv "$t" "$F";;
esac
exit 0
"""

FAKE_GH = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub: `gh pr list --json …` answers with $CC_FAKE_GH; anything else is not asked for here.
set -u
case "${1:-} ${2:-}" in "pr list") cat "$CC_FAKE_GH";; *) exit 1;; esac
"""

FAKE_DOCKER = r"""#!/usr/bin/env bash
# selfcheck stub: containers are lines "name<TAB>status<TAB>finishedAt" in $CC_FAKE_DOCKER. Speaks the three verbs
# cc-janitor uses — `ps -a` honouring its name= and status= filters, `inspect`, `rm` (which really takes the row
# out) — and nothing else: an `rmi` here is a failure, not a feature. $CC_FAKE_DOCKER_DOWN plays a daemon that is gone.
set -u; F="$CC_FAKE_DOCKER"
[ -z "${CC_FAKE_DOCKER_DOWN:-}" ] || { echo "Cannot connect to the Docker daemon" >&2; exit 1; }
cmd="${1:-}"; shift || true
case "$cmd" in
  ps)
    nre=""; st=""
    while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do case "$1" in
      --filter) case "${2:-}" in name=*) nre="${2#name=}";; status=*) st="${2#status=}";; esac; shift 2;;
      *) shift;; esac; done
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r name status fin; do
      [ -n "${name:-}" ] || continue
      [ -z "$nre" ] || grep -qE -- "$nre" <<<"$name" || continue
      [ -z "$st" ] || [ "$st" = "$status" ] || continue
      printf '%s\n' "$name"
    done < "$F";;
  inspect)
    while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do case "$1" in --format|-f) shift 2;; *) break;; esac; done
    for n in "$@"; do awk -F'\t' -v k="$n" '$1==k{printf "/%s\t%s\t%s\n",$1,$2,$3}' "$F"; done;;
  rm)
    for n in "$@"; do case "$n" in -*) continue;; esac
      t=$(mktemp); awk -F'\t' -v k="$n" '$1!=k' "$F" > "$t"; mv "$t" "$F"; done;;
  *) echo "fake docker: '$cmd' is not a verb here" >&2; exit 1;;
esac
exit 0
"""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
